  }
  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Project Architecture**\n\nI\'m currently focused on defining the project\'s core architecture and the essential data types. Specifically, I\'m working on `src/types.ts` and considering what constitutes a \"Project\" in this context. It\'ll have a name and a source which can be a zip file, folder, files, or URL. I\'m aiming for a robust and flexible foundation for the AI-powered website to Hostinger deployment converter.\n\n\n**Refining Project Analysis**\n\nI\'ve been drilling down into the project analysis component, particularly the structure of the data it generates. The plan is to output a comprehensive analysis, including framework, language, dependencies, and compatibility scores. Now, the `analysis` object includes far more data such as `database`, `apiDependencies`, `authentication` and a new `fixes` field that describes fixes, warnings, and infos for the project.\n\n\n**Expanding Analysis Data**\n\nI\'m now expanding the analysis data structure to incorporate more detailed validation results and package information. This includes storing checks, a scoring system, and counts for passes, warnings, and failures. I am also planning to store package details such as a blob, filename, size, mode, and a download URL. Also, I am thinking of including a build mode field.\n\n**Integrating Capabilities**\n\nNow I\'m integrating core capabilities for multi-source import, focusing on detailed file parsing and handling various input types. This includes ZIP archives, folders, multiple files, and public URLs, alongside preset sample projects. The deep static analysis engine in `src/utils/analyzer.ts` will parse `package.json`, and scan for environmental variables, API calls, and local host addresses to improve project analysis.\n\n\n**Developing Fix Engine**\n\nI\'m deep in the intelligent fix engine now, specifically `src/utils/fixEngine.ts`. I\'m coding fixes, scanning for origins and database clients and integrating cloud services with Stripe/PayPal keys, and designing the asset scanner that fixes broken relative links, and absolute paths like `/assets/image.png` to `assets/image.png`. I\'m developing a route scanner for Next.js and react-router. The security detector will find exposed secret keys and connection strings. I\'m focusing on various fixes such as localhost to dynamic origins, absolute path asset resolution, `.htaccess` creation, robots.txt, sitemap.xml, `.env.example`, favicon and `<link rel=\"icon\">` insertion in HTML, and Windows compatibility.\n\n\n**Implementing AI Audit**\n\nI\'m now integrating the AI deep audit feature, which will use Gemini 3.7 Flash, allowing for custom Hostinger architecture recommendations. I\'m focusing on the server-side integration that analyzes the project manifest and key code files. I\'m also adding line endings normalization and trimming, and package.json validation, including the start script, and node engine compatibility. I\'m also preparing multiple deployment targets: Static Hosting, Node.js Hosting, PHP Hosting, and Full-Stack Separated. The packager will auto-include deployment instructions and hPanel screenshots.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 4
      nanos: 891801857
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
