    thinking: "**Designing the OAuth Flow**\n\nOkay, I\'m starting to map out the GitHub OAuth flow for secure private repository imports. I need to figure out the exact steps, from user authorization to token retrieval. I\'m focusing on the integration points with GoDeploy and the necessary error handling for authentication failures.\n\n\n**Defining the Endpoint Logic**\n\nNow, I\'m fleshing out the server-side endpoint behavior. The `/api/auth/github/url` route is taking shape: it verifies environment variables and crafts the authorization URL. The callback route handles the token exchange and posts a message back to the opener. I\'m focusing on handling both configured and unconfigured client scenarios, to provide helpful error messaging.\n\n\n**Implementing the Callback Handler**\n\nI\'ve got the callback handler mostly implemented; it includes redirection back to the originating window on success and handles errors with appropriate messages. I\'m leveraging a clean HTML structure, with inline Javascript to transmit the user profile data. I\'m making sure it handles both successful and error scenarios gracefully.\n\n\n**Developing the PAT Feature**\n\nI\'m now implementing a new PAT (Personal Access Token) flow for direct account connections. The user will be able to input a PAT, validate it via GitHub\'s API, and then store it securely in local storage. This new method will automatically fetch all the user\'s repositories (public and private) and provide a rich repository picker, with filters and a search function. The user can then select and import any of their repositories. I\'m making sure to cover manual repo entry too, allowing users to enter a custom slug or URL.\n\n\n"
